<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lotus Sutra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saddharmapundarika Sutra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lotus Sutra is one of the most influential scriptures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a>, composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> around the 1st century CE. It teaches that all beings can attain Buddhahood and that the Buddha uses skilful means to lead them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumarajiva">Kumarajiva</a>'s Chinese translation of 406 became the standard version in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, and the sutra is the basis of the Tiantai and Tendai schools and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nichiren">Nichiren</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lotus-sutra.jpg" alt="Lotus Sutra" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Text inscribed by Sugawara Mitsushige · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sugawara_Mitsushige_Lotus_Sutra,_01crop.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
